## Testing Requirements

### 1. Test Coverage Expectations
- **Critical paths**: 100% coverage (auth, payments, checkout)
- **Business logic**: 80%+ coverage
- **UI components**: 70%+ coverage (focus on user interactions)
- **Utilities/helpers**: 90%+ coverage

### 2. What to Test
- ✅ User interactions (clicks, form submissions)
- ✅ Business logic (calculations, validations, transformations)
- ✅ Error handling (error states, edge cases)
- ✅ API integrations (mocked)
- ✅ Critical user flows (e2e for checkout, auth)

### 3. What NOT to Test
- ❌ Third-party library internals
- ❌ Simple getters/setters
- ❌ Implementation details (test behavior, not internals)

### 4. Test Patterns
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD: Test file naming
// UserProfile.test.tsx
// calculatePrice.test.ts

// ✅ GOOD: Test structure (AAA pattern)
describe('calculatePrice', () => {
  it('should calculate total with tax', () => {
    // Arrange
    const basePrice = 100;
    const taxRate = 0.1;
    
    // Act
    const result = calculatePrice(basePrice, taxRate);
    
    // Assert
    expect(result).toBe(110);
  });
});

// ✅ GOOD: Test user interactions
it('should submit form with valid data', async () => {
  render(<CheckoutForm />);
  
  await userEvent.type(screen.getByLabelText('Email'), 'test@example.com');
  await userEvent.click(screen.getByRole('button', { name: /submit/i }));
  
  await waitFor(() => {
    expect(mockSubmit).toHaveBeenCalledWith({ email: 'test@example.com' });
  });
});
```

### 5. Mocking Guidelines
- **Mock external dependencies**: API calls, third-party services
- **Use MSW for API mocking**: Mock Service Worker for realistic API mocks
- **Mock React Query**: Use `@tanstack/react-query` test utilities
- **Don't mock everything**: Test real implementations when possible

