## Long File & Component Organization Patterns

### 1. Component Splitting Strategy
```typescript
// ❌ BAD: 500+ line component doing everything
const HostDashboard = () => {
  // 100 lines of state
  // 200 lines of effects
  // 200 lines of render logic
};

// ✅ GOOD: Split into focused components/hooks
const HostDashboard = () => {
  const { listings, isLoading } = useHostListings();
  const { bookings } = useHostBookings();
  
  return (
    <DashboardLayout>
      <ListingsSection listings={listings} isLoading={isLoading} />
      <BookingsSection bookings={bookings} />
    </DashboardLayout>
  );
};

// Separate files:
// - useHostListings.ts (hook)
// - useHostBookings.ts (hook)
// - ListingsSection.tsx (component)
// - BookingsSection.tsx (component)
```

### 2. Large File Organization
When file grows large, split by:
- **Feature/domain**: Group related functionality
- **Concerns**: Separate data fetching, business logic, presentation
- **Lifecycle**: Separate initialization, updates, cleanup logic

```
// ❌ BAD: Everything in one file
ListingsPage.tsx (800 lines)

// ✅ GOOD: Organized by concern
ListingsPage/
  ├── index.tsx (main component, 100 lines)
  ├── hooks/
  │   ├── useListings.ts
  │   ├── useListingFilters.ts
  │   └── useListingActions.ts
  │
  ├── components/
  │   ├── ListingCard.tsx
  │   ├── ListingFilters.tsx
  │   └── ListingActions.tsx
  │
  ├── types.ts
  └── utils.ts
```

### 3. Redundant Component Detection
- **Before creating component**: Search for similar existing components
- **Check component library**: `/components/ui` and `/components/common`
- **Pattern matching**: If component is 80%+ similar, extend existing
- **Composition over duplication**: Build from smaller pieces

